body:
Hi Team. 
We noticed that calls to `od_read()` have a second argument which is the read timeout in ms. There is also a config variable `client_login_timeout` that is, presumably, a timeout on the overall (or individual read operations for) authentication process.

However, the calls to `od_read()` in [sources/auth.c](https://github.com/yandex/odyssey/blob/1e2466f41a28eb14d7de086522c1f60b9790f981/sources/auth.c) (where we still haven't verified the identity of the client) have the timeouts disabled (passed `UINT32_MAX`). [This call in `auth.c`](https://github.com/yandex/odyssey/blob/1e2466f41a28eb14d7de086522c1f60b9790f981/sources/auth.c#L602-L604):
```C
/* wait for SASLResponse */
	for (;;) {
		// TODO: here's infinite wait, need to replace it with
		// client_login_timeout
		msg = od_read(&client->io, UINT32_MAX);
        ...
    }
```
 also specifically mentions this issue.

I wanted to ask - is there a reason for this or is there any other mitigation that makes this irrelevant? I can imagine a defective (or malicious) driver that sends the auth request packet and then hangs, effectively occupying the socket/port indefinitely (as odyssey will block on the `od_read` call and never return).

This macro allows to define module-specific log functions, to make code simplier.

Just write DEFINE_SIMPLE_MODULE_LOGGER(hw, "host-watcher") at the beggining of the module file, and then write logs with functions like:

hw_log("Some message %d", 42);
hw_debug("Some debug msg");
hw_error("Some error");
hw_fatal("Guess i'll die");

This functions are equivalent of
od_log(OD_LOGGER_GLOBAL, "context from 2 arg of macro", NULL, NULL, fmt, args);
